.TH "FLOX\-ACTIVATE" "1" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
flox\-activate \- activate environments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
flox [<general\-options>] activate
     [\-d=<path> | \-r=<owner>/<name>]
     [\-t]
     [\-\-print\-script]
     [\-\-start\-services | \-\-no\-start\-services]
     [\-m=(dev|run)]
     [\-g=<generation>]
     [\-c=<shell command> | \-\- <exec command>...]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Configures a shell with everything defined by the environment:
.IP \(bu 2
Downloads packages and adds their \f[CR]bin\f[R] directories to your
\f[CR]$PATH\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Sets environment variables and aliases.
.IP \(bu 2
Runs hooks.
.IP \(bu 2
Starts services (if \f[CR]\-\-start\-services\f[R] is specified).
.PP
\f[CR]flox activate\f[R] may run in one of four modes:
.IP \(bu 2
interactive: \f[CR]flox activate\f[R] when invoked from an interactive
shell
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Launches an interactive sub\-shell.
The shell to be launched is determined by \f[CR]$FLOX_SHELL\f[R] or
\f[CR]$SHELL\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
shell command: \f[CR]flox activate \-c CMD\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Runs \f[CR]CMD\f[R] in the same environment as if run inside an
interactive shell produced by an interactive \f[CR]flox activate\f[R].
The shell \f[CR]CMD\f[R] is run by is determined by
\f[CR]$FLOX_SHELL\f[R] or \f[CR]$SHELL\f[R].
Because \f[CR]CMD\f[R] is passed to a shell, shell features like running
multiple commands with \f[CR]&&\f[R] can be used.
.IP \(bu 2
exec command: \f[CR]flox activate \-\- CMD\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Execs \f[CR]CMD\f[R] directly after performing all parts of activation
except for running scripts in \f[CR][profile]\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
in\-place: \f[CR]flox activate\f[R] when invoked from a non\-interactive
shell with its \f[CR]stdout\f[R] redirected
e.g.\ \f[CR]eval \(dq$(flox activate)\(dq\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Produces commands to be sourced by the parent shell.
Flox will determine the parent shell from \f[CR]$FLOX_SHELL\f[R] or
otherwise automatically determine the parent shell and fall back to
\f[CR]$SHELL\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]flox activate\f[R] currently supports \f[CR]bash\f[R],
\f[CR]fish\f[R], \f[CR]tcsh\f[R], and \f[CR]zsh\f[R] shells for any of
the detection mechanisms described above.
.PP
When invoked interactively, the shell prompt will be modified to display
the active environments, as shown below:
.IP
.EX
flox [env1 env2 env3] <normal prompt>
.EE
.PP
When multiple environments are activated each of their shell hooks
(\f[CR]profile\f[R] and \f[CR]hook\f[R] scripts) are executed in the
context of the environment that they come from.
This means that for each shell hook various environment variables such
as \f[CR]PATH\f[R], \f[CR]MANPATH\f[R], \f[CR]PKG_CONFIG_PATH\f[R],
\f[CR]PYTHONPATH\f[R], etc, are set to the appropriate values for the
environment in which the shell hook was defined.
See \f[B]\f[CB]manifest.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R] for more details on shell
hooks.
.PP
To reverse activation, run \f[B]\f[CB]flox\-deactivate(1)\f[B]\f[R].
Inside a \f[CR]flox activate\f[R] subshell, \f[CR]flox deactivate\f[R]
is equivalent to \f[CR]exit\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Activate Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-c <command>\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-command <command>\f[R]
Shell command string to run in a subshell started in the activated
environment
.TP
\f[CR]\-\- <command> [<arguments>]\f[R]
Command to exec in the activated environment.
This does not run any profile scripts
.TP
\f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-trust\f[R]
Trust a remote environment for this activation.
Activating an environment executes a shell hook which may execute
arbitrary code.
This presents a security risk, so you will be prompted whether to trust
the environment.
Environments owned by the current user and Flox are always trusted.
You may set certain environments to always be trusted using the config
key
\f[CR]trusted_environments.\(dq<owner/name>\(dq = (trust | deny)\f[R],
or via the following command:
\f[CR]flox config \-\-set trusted_environments.\(rs\(dq<owner/name>\(rs\(dq trust\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-print\-script\f[R]
Prints an activation script to \f[CR]stdout\f[R] that\(cqs suitable for
sourcing in a shell rather than activation via creating a subshell.
\f[CR]flox\f[R] automatically knows when to print the activation script
to \f[CR]stdout\f[R], so this command is just a debugging aid for users.
.TP
\f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-start\-services\f[R]
Start the services listed in the manifest when activating the
environment.
If no services are running, the services from the manifest will be
started, otherwise a warning will be displayed and activation will
continue.
.PP
To start services by default without requiring \f[CR]\-s\f[R], set
\f[CR]services.auto\-start = true\f[R] in the manifest.
.PP
The services started with this flag will be cleaned up once the last
activation of this environment terminates.
.PP
A remote environment can only have a single set of running services,
regardless of how many times the environment is activated concurrently.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-no\-start\-services\f[R]
Don\(cqt start services even if configured in the manifest with
\f[CR]auto\-start = true\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-m (dev|run)\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-mode (dev|run)\f[R]
Activate the environment in either \(lqdev\(rq or \(lqrun\(rq mode.
Overrides the \f[CR]options.activate.mode\f[R] setting in the manifest.
See \f[B]\f[CB]manifest.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R] for more details on activation
modes.
.TP
\f[CR]\-g <generation>\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-generation <generation>\f[R]
Activate a FloxHub environment at a specific generation.
.SS Environment Options
If no environment is specified for an environment command, the
environment in the current directory or the active environment that was
last activated is used.
.TP
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]
Path containing a .flox/ directory.
.TP
\f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-reference\f[R]
A FloxHub environment, specified in the form \f[CR]<owner>/<name>\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-D\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-default\f[R]
Use your default environment (\f[CR]<your\-user>/default\f[R]).
When unauthenticated in an interactive context, you will be prompted to
log in.
In non\-interactive contexts (e.g., scripts or CI), this flag will fail
with an error when authentication is missing.
.SS General Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
Prints help information.
.PP
The following options can be passed when running any \f[CR]flox\f[R]
subcommand but must be specified \f[I]before\f[R] the subcommand.
.TP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase logging verbosity.
Invoke multiple times for increasing detail.
.TP
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Silence logs except for errors.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.SS Variables set by \f[CR]flox activate\f[R]
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_ENV\f[R]
Contains the path to the built environment.
This directory contains a merged set of \f[CR]bin\f[R], \f[CR]lib\f[R],
etc directories for all the packages in the environment.
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_PROMPT_ENVIRONMENTS\f[R]
Contains a space\-delimited list of the active environments,
e.g.\ \f[CR]owner1/foo owner2/bar local_env\f[R].
If \f[CR]hide_default_prompt\f[R] is set to \f[CR]true\f[R],
environments named \f[CR]default\f[R] are excluded.
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_ENV_CACHE\f[R]
\f[CR]activate\f[R] sets this variable to a directory that can be used
by an environment\(cqs hook to store transient files.
These files will persist for environments used locally, but they will
not be pushed, and they will not persist when using a remote environment
with \f[CR]\-r\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_ENV_PROJECT\f[R]
\f[CR]activate\f[R] sets this variable to the directory of the project
using the Flox environment.
For environments stored locally, this is the directory containing the
environment.
When running \f[CR]flox activate \-r\f[R], this is set to the current
working directory.
This variable can be used to find project files in environment hooks.
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_ENV_DESCRIPTION\f[R]
\f[CR]activate\f[R] sets this variable to the project name of the
environment.
It can be used to identify or construct messages about the environment.
.TP
\f[CR]$_FLOX_ACTIVE_ENVIRONMENTS\f[R]
A JSON array containing one object per active environment.
This is currently an implementation detail and its contents are subject
to change.
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_ACTIVATE_START_SERVICES\f[R]
\f[CR]\(dqtrue\(dq\f[R] if this activation started services,
\f[CR]\(dqfalse\(dq\f[R] otherwise.
.SS Variables used by \f[CR]flox activate\f[R]
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_SHELL\f[R], \f[CR]$SHELL\f[R]
When activating an environment Flox will either launch a sub\-shell or
emit commands to configure an already\-running (parent) shell.
In both of these cases Flox needs to know which shell to use, and these
variables are used to control the selection process.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
interactive and command modes: When launching a sub\-shell Flox will
invoke the shell specified in \f[CR]$FLOX_SHELL\f[R] if set or fall back
to invoke \f[CR]$SHELL\f[R] by default.
.IP \(bu 2
in\-place mode: When performing an \(lqin place\(rq activation Flox will
attempt to detect its parent shell type unless overridden by the
\f[CR]$FLOX_SHELL\f[R] variable, and if it cannot detect its parent
shell type then will produce a script with syntax determined by
\f[CR]$SHELL\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[CR]$FLOX_PROMPT_COLOR_{1,2}\f[R]
Flox adds text to the beginning of the shell prompt to indicate which
environments are active.
A set of default colors are used to color this prompt, but the colors
may be overridden with the \f[CR]$FLOX_PROMPT_COLOR_1\f[R] and
\f[CR]$FLOX_PROMPT_COLOR_2\f[R] environment variables.
.RS
.PP
The values of these variables should be integers chosen from the
256\-color palette as described in the \f[B]xterm\-256color
chart\f[R] <\f[I]https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Xterm_256color_chart.svg\f[R]>.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Activate an environment stored in the current directory:
.IP
.EX
flox activate
.EE
.PP
Activate an environment \f[CR]some_user/myenv\f[R] that\(cqs been pushed
to FloxHub:
.IP
.EX
flox activate \-r some_user/myenv
.EE
.PP
Invoke a command inside an environment without entering its subshell:
.IP
.EX
flox activate \-\- cmd \-\-some\-arg arg1 arg2
.EE
.PP
Activate \f[CR]default\f[R] Flox environment only within the current
shell (add to the relevant \(lqrc\(rq file,
e.g.\ \f[CR]\(ti/.bashrc\f[R] or \f[CR]\(ti/.zprofile\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
eval \(dq$(flox activate)\(dq
.EE
